%[text] # Guided lab: Visual Anomaly Detector
%[text] Hands-on workshop | MATLAB R2026b | about 25 to 30 minutes
%[text] Work through the sections in order using **Run Section**. This guide opens the R2026b app with the PillQC images supplied in the same folder (real photos of pills with chip and dirt defects). The MathWorks screenshots use the same PillQC data set, so your screen should look very similar; exact scores can differ slightly because the split and training are random.
%[text] Required products: Visual Inspection Toolbox, Image Processing Toolbox, Deep Learning Toolbox, and the Deep Learning Toolbox Model for ResNet-18 Network support package. Training speed depends on the classroom computer.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Lab steps"}
%%
%[text] ## Step 1: Check the supplied images
%[text] Set MATLAB's **Current Folder** to the folder containing this guide. Keep the `dataset` folder beside it. The training set has only normal pills; calibration and test each contain normal, chip, and dirt images.
%[text] ![PillQC sample images: normal, chip, dirt](text:image:aa01)
labRoot = pwd;
dataRoot = fullfile(labRoot,"dataset");
assert(isfolder(dataRoot),"Open the folder containing this guide and dataset first.");
imdsTrain = imageDatastore(fullfile(dataRoot,"train"),IncludeSubfolders=true,LabelSource="foldernames");
imdsCal = imageDatastore(fullfile(dataRoot,"calibration"),IncludeSubfolders=true,LabelSource="foldernames");
imdsTest = imageDatastore(fullfile(dataRoot,"test"),IncludeSubfolders=true,LabelSource="foldernames");
countEachLabel(imdsTrain)
countEachLabel(imdsCal)
countEachLabel(imdsTest)
%[text] **Check:** training = 104 normal; calibration = 15 normal, 13 chip, 41 dirt; test = 30 normal, 30 chip, 97 dirt (all different images). Keep test images out of threshold tuning.
%%
%[text] ## Step 2: Open Visual Anomaly Detector
%[text] In the **Apps** gallery, find **Image Processing and Computer Vision**, then select **Visual Anomaly Detector**. You can also run the command below to open the app with our pre-partitioned images already loaded. Run this section once.
%[text] ![App location in the MATLAB gallery](text:image:aa02)
anomalyClasses = ["chip","dirt"];
visualAnomalyDetector(imdsTrain,imdsCal,imdsTest,anomalyClasses)
%[text] **Check:** the app opens with 104 training images and separate calibration and test images. If the app does not open, check the installed products and ResNet-18 support package.
%%
%[text] ## Step 3: Browse the imported data
%[text] In the app, select **Visualize**. Switch among **Training Data**, **Calibration Data**, and **Test Data**. Look for the broken edge of chipped pills and the brown dirt particles. Also notice the background colour of each class. Close Browse Data when you are finished.
%[text] ![Browse Data tab (MathWorks PillQC example)](text:image:aa03)
%[text] **Think:** why do we train PatchCore with normal examples but retain labeled anomalies for calibration and evaluation?
%%
%[text] ## Step 4: Train a PatchCore detector
%[text] The **DETECTOR** tab is organised left to right in the order you work: **FILE** (New/Open/Save Session) → **DATA** (Visualize) → **DETECTORS** (choose a model) → **TRAINING** (Train, Set Threshold) → **ANALYZE** (Confusion Matrix, ROC Curve and, under the small arrow, Histogram, Precision-Recall, Summary) → **EXPLAIN** (Classification Decision) → **COMPARE** (Compare Trained Detectors) → **LAYOUT** → **EXPORT**. Grey buttons are not broken: each one switches on only when the step before it is done.
%[text] ![DETECTOR tab with the StudentTeacher hyperparameters (default selection)](text:image:aa10)
%[text] The app selects **StudentTeacher** by default (status *Untrained*). The gallery offers four anomaly detectors:
%[text] - **StudentTeacher**: a small *student* network learns to copy a pretrained *teacher* on normal images; where they disagree is anomalous. Trains for several epochs.
%[text] - **PatchCore**: stores features of small image patches from normal images in a memory bank; a new patch far from every stored patch is anomalous. No epochs, fast on CPU, works with few images. **We use this one.**
%[text] - **FastFlow**: learns the probability distribution of normal features; unlikely regions are anomalous.
%[text] - **FCDD**: a light fully convolutional model that pulls normal features towards a centre; it can also use some real or synthetic anomaly images.
%[text] Click **PatchCore**. The **Hyperparameters** panel changes to the PatchCore settings (pretrained backbone, default ResNet-18; compression ratio, default 0.1 = keep 10% of patch features). Keep the defaults and click **Train**. Wait until the Detector Browser shows **Trained (No Threshold)**. On a typical laptop CPU this takes under a minute.
%[text] ![PatchCore training screen (MathWorks PillQC example)](text:image:aa04)
%[text] **Check:** the detector is trained but has no threshold yet, so **Set Threshold** is now enabled while **Classification Decision** and **Compare Trained Detectors** stay grey. If training cannot finish during class, ask the instructor to show the saved session and continue with the discussion questions.
%%
%[text] ## Step 5: Set a threshold
%[text] Click **Set Threshold**. In the **Threshold** panel set **Predefined Threshold** to **Max F1 Score** and keep the data switch on **Calibration Data**, not *Calibration and Test Data*. The threshold turns anomaly scores into normal or anomalous decisions. Once it is set, the status changes to **Trained** and the ANALYZE, EXPLAIN and COMPARE buttons switch on.
%[text] ![Threshold panel (MathWorks PillQC example)](text:image:aa05)
%[text] **Think:** how would lowering the threshold change false alarms and missed defects?
%%
%[text] ## Step 6: Evaluate on held-out test images
%[text] Open **Confusion Matrix** and **Summary**. Read the number of correct and incorrect decisions for the test set. Do not retune the threshold on these test results.
%[text] ![Confusion matrix (MathWorks PillQC example)](text:image:aa06)
%[text] **Record:** your test-set true positives, true negatives, false positives, and false negatives. Compare your numbers with the screenshot and with your neighbour.
%%
%[text] ## Step 7: Inspect individual decisions
%[text] Click **Classification Decision**. Filter to one result group, then inspect a normal part and a defective part. Find a false positive or false negative if one exists.
%[text] ![Classification Decision tab (MathWorks PillQC example)](text:image:aa07)
%[text] **Think:** did the model react to the intended defect, or to harmless lighting, background, or position differences?
%%
%[text] ## Step 8: Normalize, then show the anomaly heatmap
%[text] Stay in the **VIEW DECISION** tab (opened by **Classification Decision** in Step 7). Do these two clicks **in this order**:
%[text] 1. **Normalize Anomaly Score** (the first button, in the **NORMALIZE** group at the far left). A dialog opens with two settings: the *normal image percentile range* and the *normalized output range of a normal image*. Keep the default values and click **OK**.
%[text] 2. **Show Heatmap** (in the **HEATMAP** group, next to it). The heatmap now appears on every thumbnail. \
%[text] **Why normalize first?** Without it, each image's colours are stretched to that image's own minimum and maximum, so even a perfect pill shows red spots. Normalizing uses the scores of the normal calibration images as one common scale: a normal pill then stays blue/clear and only real defects turn yellow/red, so you can compare colours between images.
%[text] ![Without normalization: colours stretched per image (MathWorks PillQC example)](text:image:aa08)
%[text] ![On one common scale: normal pills stay clear, defects light up (test run on this lab data)](text:image:aa09)
%[text] Then use the **HEATMAP** controls: **Blend Type** (how the colour mixes with the photo), **Colormap** (colour scheme) and **Opacity** (lower it to see the pill under the colour). Select a chip or dirt image and check where the heat is.
%[text] **Record:** one image where the heatmap points to the defect and one image that needs closer review. The heatmap localizes unusual appearance; it does not establish the physical cause.
%%
%[text] ## Finish and save your work
%[text] On the app's **Detector** tab, choose **Save Session \> Save As** and save your session under your own filename. The instructor can also export the detector or generated training function from **Export**.
%[text] Discuss which new camera angle, lighting condition, background, or pill type would require more normal training images. PillQC is a small teaching data set; results on it do not establish production inspection accuracy.
%[text] Interface screenshots for steps 3 through 8: [MathWorks R2026b app walkthrough](https://www.mathworks.com/help/visual-inspection/ug/train-anomaly-detection-model-using-visual-anomaly-detector.html). The Apps gallery screenshot was supplied for this workshop. Sample images: PillQC data set (Taylor, 2022, https://github.com/matlab-deep-learning/pillQC).

